<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of the Cross</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a>–<a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of the Cross was a Spanish Carmelite friar, priest and mystic who worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a> to reform the Carmelite order. Imprisoned by opponents of the reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1577–1578, he composed there part of his Spiritual Canticle. His poems and commentaries, including The Dark Night of the Soul, are among the summits of Spanish literature and Christian mysticism. He was canonised in 1726.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross.jpg" alt="John of the Cross" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zurbar%C3%A1n_(atribuido)-John_of_the_Cross-1656.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John of the Cross was born Juan de Yepes y Álvarez at Fontiveros, a town of about 2,000 people near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>. His father, Gonzalo, a weaver who had been rejected by his family for marrying below him, died around 1545, and the family lived in poverty. His mother, Catalina, moved with her sons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a> in 1551, where John entered a school for 160 poor children and later studied the humanities at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> school from 1559 to 1563. In 1563 he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelite Order</a>, and in 1564 he enrolled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> to study theology and philosophy. He was ordained a priest in 1567.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a> in 1567 John met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a>, who persuaded him to delay joining the Carthusian order and to follow her reform, which sought to restore the Primitive Rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelite Order</a>. On 28 November 1568 he founded a monastery for friars at Duruelo and took the name John of the Cross. After tensions among the Carmelites, a General Chapter at Piacenza in 1576 ordered the suppression of the Discalced houses.</p><p>On the night of 2 December 1577 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a> opposed to reform seized John in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, where a court of friars sentenced him to imprisonment. He was lashed publicly at least weekly and held in a cell barely 10 by 6 feet. There he composed a great part of the Spiritual Canticle, and he escaped through a small window on 15 August 1578.</p><p>In 1580 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a> authorised the separation of the Discalced Carmelites. John became a leader of the new order and in 1585 Vicar Provincial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, founding seven monasteries there. After disagreeing with the order's leadership he was sent to La Peñuela in June 1591, fell ill and died of erysipelas at Úbeda on 14 December 1591. He was beatified in 1675, canonised in 1726 and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1926.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>John's poems add up to fewer than 2,500 verses, and his theological works are largely commentaries on them. The Spiritual Canticle, in which the bride, representing the soul, searches for the bridegroom, Christ, is seen as a free Spanish version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a>; its first 31 stanzas date from 1578, and it survives in versions of 39 and 40 stanzas. The Dark Night narrates the soul's journey to union with God through detachment, and the Ascent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a>, composed between 1581 and 1585, is a systematic study of that ascetical path. His works were first published in 1618, and his writing influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a>, Thérèse de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisieux">Lisieux</a> and Thomas Merton.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John of the Cross” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the chief power of the Catholic Counter-Reformation: Ignatius of Loyola founded the Society of Jesus, Teresa of Ávila and <b>John of the Cross</b> reformed the Carmelites, and Spanish theologians shaped the Council of Trent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Teresa of Ávila and <b>John of the Cross</b>, Spanish mystics and reformers of the Carmelite Order, taught interior conversion and deeper prayer; Thomas Merton called John of the Cross the greatest of all mystical theologians.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fontiveros, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Úbeda, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.93,-4.97&amp;year=1573" aria-label="Fontiveros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4428 703 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John of the Cross"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4878 973h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4878 973h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.93,-4.97&amp;year=1573">What was here in 1573? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
